d is a privative or functional-state noun (deficiency, absence, lack, deficient activity) and the complement is a gene product (enzyme, complement component, or gene symbol). The phrase has no disease-category head (\"disease\", \"syndrome\") and no eponym, so the model treats it as a compositional biochemical description and tags it O.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** The True Positives are detected because their spans end in a nosological head (\"...disease\", \"...syndrome\", \"...cancer\", \"...retardation\") or are eponymous. These are conventional disease labels, not open-class descriptions of a molecule's state.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label the full noun phrase as SpecificDisease when all three conditions hold:\n  1. The head is a privative state noun or adjective: deficiency, absence, lack, or deficient activity.\n  2. The complement is a named enzyme, protein, complement component, or gene symbol.\n  3. The context frames the phrase as an organism-level pathological condition, such as \"caused by a deficiency of\", \"patient with absence of\", or \"responsible for enzyme deficiency\".\n\n  The span should include the privative head and its function words (\"deficiency of\", \"absence of\"), not only the molecule name. The molecule alone is not the disease. Do not tag the phrase when it describes a molecular mechanism, such as \"a deficiency of norrin\" in 10484772, and flag that case as an annotation inconsistency to resolve.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4327, "output_tokens": 5989, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 4936}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}